From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_UNSWIZZLE_THREADS=<n>: large unswizzles are split by lines over helper threads (Lichii PR #573, as lethevimlet/oot-bc250 f47ae1e92a)

A new texture's block-linear data is made plain on the thread that needs it. In Ocarina's fly-over
that is 6-9% of the back GPU thread (stack samples of 10 Nov), and 377 ms of the 1,040 ms door
frame (EDEN_UP_HITCH_TRACE, 587 images, 1.6 GiB). With the switch, an unswizzle of 1 MiB or more
is split by lines over n helper threads (1 to 4) plus the caller, which waits for all of them. Each
line writes its own bytes of the output, so the result is the same.

Upstream has this always on with a quarter of the host threads, at most 4. Here: off unless the
switch is set, no thread is made without it, and the number is the switch's. The helpers are named
"Unswizzle"; on the PS5 they go where the front puts named secondary threads (CPUs 7 and 9-12
unless EDEN_PS5_PLACE names them), away from the game's cores.

The loop body is unchanged; only its two loops became one over (slice, line).

